counterfactuals.output:
An optical characterization system and method are disclosed for generating a spatially resolved representation of degradation in a photon-emitting or photon-transmitting article. Flux degradation data associated with a plurality of spatial locations of the article are acquired over at least one measurement interval. The flux degradation data are spatially processed to produce a spatial flux-degradation distribution. A change in photon spectrum is calculated from spectral measurement data by determining, for a plurality of wavelength bands, a difference between a measured photon spectrum and a reference photon spectrum. The calculated photon spectral change is mapped to corresponding spatial locations to generate a spatially dependent spectral-change distribution. The spatial flux-degradation distribution and the spatially dependent spectral-change distribution are combined according to a predetermined correlation, weighting, or transformation function to produce a composite spatial degradation output representative of both photon-flux variation and spectral variation across the article.

In one embodiment, a processor is configured to normalize the flux degradation data, interpolate the normalized data over a spatial coordinate system, convert the photon spectral change into wavelength-resolved values associated with respective spatial coordinates, and combine the resulting distributions on a pixel-by-pixel or region-by-region basis. The composite spatial degradation output may comprise a map, multidimensional data structure, degradation index, corrected emission spectrum, or predicted remaining-useful-life parameter.

**Exemplary independent claim:**

1. A computer-implemented method comprising:  
   acquiring flux degradation data corresponding to a plurality of spatial locations of an optical article;  
   spatially processing the flux degradation data to generate a spatial flux-degradation distribution;  
   calculating a photon spectral change relative to a reference photon spectrum;  
   converting the photon spectral change into a spatially dependent spectral-change distribution; and  
   combining the spatial flux-degradation distribution with the spatially dependent spectral-change distribution to generate a composite spatial degradation representation of the optical article.
